{"entry_id": "327928d2-cd60-4932-a7da-06e811d76aeb", "session_id": "ffd50e11-14a1-420a-8ca7-1bdbe6046ecf", "user_id": "d976e6a6-dcee-4072-859d-0c36b07e89c8", "timestamp": "2026-04-29T02:16:15.674473", "query": "When does open enrollment end?", "route": "hr_in_scope", "escalated": false, "situation_facts": "", "chunks_used": [{"document_name": "Nexarion 2025 Benefits Guide", "section_header": "5.1 Premium Payment During Unpaid Leave", "chunk_index": 30}, {"document_name": "Nexarion 2025 Benefits Guide", "section_header": "Section 5: Benefits During Leaves of Absence", "chunk_index": 28}, {"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "NEXARION SOLUTIONS, INC.", "chunk_index": 14}, {"document_name": "Nexarion 2025 Benefits Guide", "section_header": "7.1 Open Enrollment Window", "chunk_index": 39}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Remote Work Technology Supplement", "section_header": "3.3 Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) Policy", "chunk_index": 30}], "final_answer": "The open enrollment period for the plan year 2026 will end on November 14, 2025, at 11:59 PM ET. You must submit your elections in the WEX Health portal by this deadline. If you do not submit your elections, they will default to the prior year's elections for medical, dental, and vision plans. Additionally, please note that contributions for Flexible Spending Accounts (FSA) and Health Savings Accounts (HSA) do not auto-renew, so you will need to actively elect your contribution amounts for the upcoming year.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n1. Nexarion 2025 Benefits Guide \u2014 5.1 Premium Payment During Unpaid Leave\n2. Nexarion 2025 Benefits Guide \u2014 Section 5: Benefits During Leaves of Absence\n3. Nexarion 2025 Benefits Guide \u2014 7.1 Open Enrollment Window\n4. Nexarion Remote Work Technology Supplement \u2014 3.3 Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) Policy", "grounding_score": 1.0, "compliance_passed": true}
{"entry_id": "5cb14d84-47a9-44d2-982f-4eb8325b1a8e", "session_id": "ffd50e11-14a1-420a-8ca7-1bdbe6046ecf", "user_id": "d976e6a6-dcee-4072-859d-0c36b07e89c8", "timestamp": "2026-04-29T02:16:50.590853", "query": "How much PTO do I get in a year?", "route": "hr_in_scope", "escalated": false, "situation_facts": "", "chunks_used": [{"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "8.2 Parental Leave", "chunk_index": 69}, {"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "1.1 PTO Accrual & Carryover - California Override", "chunk_index": 5}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "Section 14: Acknowledgment of Receipt", "chunk_index": 103}, {"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "2.3 New York Paid Family Leave (NY PFL)", "chunk_index": 19}, {"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "4.1 Washington Paid Family & Medical Leave (WA PFML)", "chunk_index": 26}], "final_answer": "Based on the retrieved policy, the amount of Paid Time Off (PTO) you receive annually depends on your length of service and employment status. Here are the details:\n\n- If you are a Full-Time Regular Employee (FTR):\n  - With 0-1 year of service: 10 days (80 hours)\n  - With 1-5 years of service: 15 days (120 hours)\n  - With 5+ years of service: 20 days (160 hours)\n\n- If you are a Part-Time Regular Employee (PTR):\n  - With 0-1 year of service: 5 days (40 hours)\n  - With 1-5 years of service: 10 days (80 hours)\n  - With 5+ years of service: 15 days (120 hours)\n\nAdditionally, you can carry over unused PTO to the next calendar year, but be aware that there is a cap on the amount that can be carried over, and any unused PTO above that cap will be forfeited on January 1 of the following year.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n1. Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1) \u2014 8.2 Parental Leave\n2. Nexarion State Policy Addendum \u2014 1.1 PTO Accrual & Carryover - California Override\n3. Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1) \u2014 Section 14: Acknowledgment of Receipt\n4. Nexarion State Policy Addendum \u2014 2.3 New York Paid Family Leave (NY PFL)\n5. Nexarion State Policy Addendum \u2014 4.1 Washington Paid Family & Medical Leave (WA PFML)", "grounding_score": 0.5, "compliance_passed": true}
{"entry_id": "0588aaf4-3bb4-438a-9bce-2db35f8adfdb", "session_id": "ffd50e11-14a1-420a-8ca7-1bdbe6046ecf", "user_id": "d976e6a6-dcee-4072-859d-0c36b07e89c8", "timestamp": "2026-04-29T02:23:30.443957", "query": "You: I'm a California-based employee and I've accumulated 28 days of PTO. My annual accrual is 18 days (I'm in my 4th year). My manager told me anything over 26 days gets forfeited on January 1. Is that right?", "route": "hr_in_scope", "escalated": false, "situation_facts": "", "chunks_used": [{"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "1.1 PTO Accrual & Carryover - California Override", "chunk_index": 5}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "NEXARION SOLUTIONS, INC.", "chunk_index": 61}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "Section 14: Acknowledgment of Receipt", "chunk_index": 103}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "NEXARION SOLUTIONS, INC.", "chunk_index": 60}, {"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "1.1 PTO Accrual & Carryover - California Override", "chunk_index": 6}], "final_answer": "You are correct that there is a cap on PTO accrual for California employees. Since you have accumulated 28 days of PTO and your annual accrual is 18 days, you have exceeded the accrual cap of 27 days (1.5 times your annual accrual). This means that further accrual of PTO will pause until your balance drops below this cap. \n\nYou will not forfeit any of your accrued PTO, as California law prohibits the forfeiture of earned wages, including PTO. However, you should plan to use some of your PTO soon to avoid reaching a point where you cannot accrue any additional days. If you do not use some of your PTO, you will remain at 28 days until you take time off, which will allow you to start accruing again once your balance is below the cap.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n1. Nexarion State Policy Addendum \u2014 1.1 PTO Accrual & Carryover - California Override\n2. Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1) \u2014 Section 14: Acknowledgment of Receipt", "grounding_score": 1.0, "compliance_passed": false}
{"entry_id": "0289597e-5a71-4f1c-8a35-b99feaff22d1", "session_id": "ffd50e11-14a1-420a-8ca7-1bdbe6046ecf", "user_id": "d976e6a6-dcee-4072-859d-0c36b07e89c8", "timestamp": "2026-04-29T02:30:26.891479", "query": "I'm a California-based employee and I've accumulated 28 days of PTO. My annual accrual is 18 days (I'm in my 4th year). My manager told me anything over 26 days gets forfeited on January 1. Is that right?", "route": "hr_in_scope", "escalated": false, "situation_facts": "", "chunks_used": [{"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "1.1 PTO Accrual & Carryover - California Override", "chunk_index": 5}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "NEXARION SOLUTIONS, INC.", "chunk_index": 61}, {"document_name": "Nexarion State Policy Addendum", "section_header": "Section 1: California (CA)", "chunk_index": 4}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "2.2 Equal Opportunity Employment", "chunk_index": 12}, {"document_name": "Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1)", "section_header": "Section 14: Acknowledgment of Receipt", "chunk_index": 103}], "final_answer": "You are correct that there is an accrual cap on PTO for California employees, which is set at 1.5 times your annual accrual rate. Since your annual accrual is 18 days, the cap for you is 27 days. You currently have 28 days of PTO, which means you have exceeded the cap by 1 day. \n\nHowever, California law prohibits the forfeiture of earned wages, including PTO. This means you will not lose any of your accrued PTO, but you will not be able to accrue any additional PTO until your balance drops below the cap of 27 days. \n\nTo avoid reaching a point where you cannot accrue any more PTO, you should consider using some of your accrued days soon. This will allow you to start accruing again once your balance is below the cap. Make sure to communicate with your manager about your plans to use your PTO.\n\n---\n